[Music]  the first modern humans that we know  have ever existed lived in africa and  they date to somewhere between 230 000  and 315 000 years ago so modern people  have been around in africa for a very  long time and then sometime around 80  000 years ago it looks like there's this  kind of big movement out of africa but  meantime on the continent of africa a  lot was happening too and that's when  you actually start to see the first  evidence that there was this really  widespread mingling and moving and in  terms of what we find archaeologically  this is when you get a real explosion in  the first art the first clothing the  first personal ornaments and most of  that happens first in africa and all of  that continues to kind of grow and  develop and change in the various parts  of africa but we don't know as much  about it as we do other regions because  there just hasn't been that much  research that's been done i first went  into the mount hora area in northern  malawi in 2016 because i wanted to see  what the potential would be to start a  new project in that region i knew  somebody had been excavating there in  1950 and that not a lot had happened  since then  so i was interested in the possibility  of actually being able to retrieve  ancient dna from this area and i did put  that in some proposals which my  colleagues reviewed and some of those  reviewed said that it wouldn't be  possible that ancient dna would not  preserve in these tropical environments  or that the site wouldn't actually be  very old and it's been rewarding to be  able to see that both of those things  are not true the site is actually quite  old it goes back into the last ice age  and some of the ancient dna that we've  been able to recover from there is some  of the oldest in africa  so a rock shelter is an area that's kind  of like an overhang and people will take  refuge under there and they might build  fires or bring home stuff that they've  hunted or whatever and so there's some  living activities that happen there but  they also will often choose those same  places as places of burial and what we  ended up discovering was all of the food  remains and the daily today activities  are recorded in these rock shelters but  we also found some human remains and  some of those human remains ended up  being some of the oldest that have been  found in the area and they are the  oldest to actually preserve ancient dna  in sub-saharan africa so what we did  with that was we combined it with a lot  of other new data with other teams that  had been working on other parts of that  part of africa eastern africa and we  found that when you kind of combine all  of these data together it really starts  to tell a big picture story about what  the ancestral population structure  looked like long ago it's interesting to  to think about the time scale because  the actual individuals that we found are  in they range in age from between about  16 000 years ago and 14 000 years ago at  the site that we discovered but there  are also other individuals in the study  and they ranged in time all the way up  to 2000 years ago every one of those  individuals can be thought of as the  product of all of their ancestors and  all of their ancestors ancestors what  we've been able to find  by doing this is that it looks like  people were really doing a lot of  widespread movement they're kind of  moving across the landscape they're  meeting their mating partners and then  of course the product is a new person  and  that ends up resulting in all of these  people who live in lots of different  places but they have  they have this kind of widespread  genetic signal and then this interesting  thing happens you start to notice that  genetically as well as in terms of the  material culture so this the stuff that  they're leaving behind it looks like  people are kind of sticking closer to  home  people were making ostrich eggshell  beads in similar ways across eastern and  southern africa and then after about 30  000 years ago there's divergence in the  styles that they make and the hypothesis  is that there was actually sort of a  barrier to the movement of people around  30 000 years ago  and so that's actually exactly the same  pattern that we see genetically where  there's evidence of widespread  movement and an actual contact between  people and then after about 30 or 20  thousand years ago right at the peak of  the last ice age those territories get  smaller and people start to behave much  more locally so we're actually seeing it  in both the dna and the and the  archaeological materials too this has  required expertise from researchers from  almost 20 different countries to do this  study and the individuals that were  presenting here the ancient individuals  come from recent archaeological  excavations such as the one i did but  also from  individuals that had been held in  museums and so some of my colleagues  were making their contributions through  sampling of those individuals and what  that means is that you have to go to  museums and you have to be able to find  what it is that you're looking for and  you need to be able to know that it is  in good condition it shows us how we can  connect the archaeological record to the  genetic record and that's one of the  things we've done in this paper that  we're really proud of is  having archaeologists co-write it with  geneticists side by side through the  whole thing  i think one of the biggest lessons here  is that we should always expect to be  surprised  [Music]  [Music]  you

